## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design low-latency scheduling primitives, memory ordering, and reclamation strategies under NUMA. Timer wheels stress correctness under high update rates and require careful ABA avoidance.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free data structures
- Multi-level timer wheels and deadline granularity
- Memory ordering and fences
- Per-slot sequence counters to prevent ABA
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse
- NUMA locality considerations and spill paths

## Code Example

```cpp
// Pseudo enqueue (illustrative)
bool enqueue_timer(timer_t* t){
  uint32_t lvl = choose_level(t->expiry);
  slot_t* s = &wheel[lvl].slots[slot_index(lvl, t->expiry)];
  timer_t* expected = nullptr;
  if (std::atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&s->payload, &expected, t)) {
    s->seq.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    return true;
  }
  // spill to overflow (simplified)
  return spill_to_overflow(t);
}
```

